## "SHIPPED" CLAIMS REQUIRE EXECUTION ARTIFACTS

This is the single most important Cascade discipline rule.

**`tsc --noEmit` clean is NOT the same as "the code ran."**
**"File parses" is NOT the same as "the browser rendered it correctly."**
**"Tests pass" is NOT the same as "the feature works."**

Before claiming "shipped," you must have:
- Run the actual code or loaded the actual page
- Captured execution output (screenshot, console output, state values)
- Verified the runtime behavior matches the brief's intended outcome

If you cannot run the code in your environment for some reason, say so explicitly:
"Code compiles clean and matches the brief's specification. I cannot run this against the live state because [reason]. Operator verification required at Layer 3 before this can be considered shipped."

That's an honest report. Claiming "shipped" when you only verified compilation is not.

