## VOICE CANON DISCIPLINE

Gamer Ascension uses the manuscript-celestial register. Reverent, mythic, precise. Never tech-bro. Never casual. Never exclamatory.

### Typography lock
- Cinzel / Cinzel Decorative for headings
- Crimson Pro for body and invitation copy
- Tangerine for Atlas annotations
- These don't get swapped without explicit operator decision in Architecture & Decisions chat

### Banned words (apply to all rendered customer-facing copy)

| Banned | Reason |
|---|---|
| "actually" | emphasis-by-contrast hedge |
| "honestly" | emphasis-by-contrast hedge |
| "truly" | emphasis-by-contrast hedge |
| "genuinely" | emphasis-by-contrast hedge |
| "really" (as intensifier) | emphasis-by-contrast hedge |
| "essentially" | filler |
| "basically" | filler |
| "of course" | self-validating transition |
| "obviously" | condescending |
| "It is worth noting" | self-validating transition |
| "Notably" | self-validating transition |
| "Importantly" | self-validating transition |
| em dashes (—) for multi-idea load | breaks reverent flow |
| Exclamation marks | breaks reverent register |
| "Awesome!" / "Great!" / "Amazing!" | motivational, breaks register |
| "Sup" / "Yo" / "Hey" / "What's up" | casual, breaks register |
| Tech-bro lexicon ("scaling," "leverage," "synergy," "stack") | wrong register |

If you find yourself reaching for a banned word, the sentence needs to be rewritten, not de-banned with synonyms.

### Em dashes are banned anywhere in copy
For ranges, use "X to Y" or "between X and Y." For asides, use two sentences. The reverent register doesn't carry em dashes.

### Iridescent violet vocabulary is RESERVED
"Iridescent," "iridescence," and similar violet/peacock vocabulary are reserved for substance moments only (rank-up ceremonies, Pool-pour climaxes, Anchored apotheosis, etc.). Using them in non-substance contexts dilutes the rank-up payoff. Do not use them in incidental UI text.

### Voice canon scan
Before reporting any copy work complete, scan your output for:
- Banned words from the table above
- Em dashes
- Exclamation marks
- Motivational language
- Tech-bro lexicon
- Iridescent violet vocabulary in non-substance contexts
- Reserved capitalized terms lowercased or renamed

If any of these surface in your output, fix them before reporting.

